<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements. Notarization rejects any build without the
  hardened runtime (`codesign --options runtime`), and the hardened runtime then
  denies the four things the BEAM cannot run without. Each one below is a hole
  punched back through it on purpose.

  This file is referenced from TWO places, deliberately pointed at the same path
  so they can never drift:

    - tauri.conf.json -> bundle.macOS.entitlements  (the .app itself)
    - scripts/codesign_release.sh                   (every Mach-O in the OTP tree)

  The second one is the whole reason this file is not just the shell's business.
  **Entitlements do not inherit across process boundaries.** `beam.smp` is
  spawned as a separate process out of Contents/Resources/ (see
  desktop/tauri/src/main.rs, resolve_release_binary) and does NOT inherit the
  shell's entitlements — it must carry these on its own signature. Sign the ERTS
  without them and the failure is the worst shape available: notarization
  passes, the DMG ships, and the VM dies at launch on the user's machine. It
  fails nowhere in our pipeline and everywhere in theirs.

  Set matched to Livebook's — a Tauri v2 app shipping an Elixir/OTP release,
  i.e. the same architecture, already notarized and in the wild. See
  daily-growth/roadmaps/LAUNCH_ROADMAP.md section III.E.

  NOTHING ELSE BELONGS HERE. In particular `com.apple.security.get-task-allow`
  (the debug entitlement) is a hard notary failure; it must never appear.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- BEAM has had a real JIT (BeamAsm) since OTP 24. It needs W^X executable
       mappings, which the hardened runtime refuses by default. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Required in practice alongside allow-jit for BEAM's code allocation.
       BeamAsm's allocator does not confine itself to the MAP_JIT regions
       allow-jit alone permits. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Load-bearing, and the one most likely to be dropped as redundant. The
       BEAM dlopen()s its NIFs at runtime — crypto.so, sqlite3_nif.so,
       asn1rt_nif.so and the rest. To the loader a NIF is a plug-in from another
       team, and library validation rejects it. Seven of those .so files do not
       even carry an execute bit, which is why codesign_release.sh finds Mach-O
       by content rather than by mode. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- OTP's launcher scripts rely on DYLD_*-driven dyld behaviour, which the
       hardened runtime scrubs from the environment unless this is set. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
